hi there this is Steve Kaufman founder  of Lync and today I'm going to talk  about the fifth secret to successful  language learning again you'll remember  that so far we've discussed the need to  spend a lot of time enough time now are  they for quite a long period of time the  second secret was to do things that  you'd like to do in the language the  third secret was to develop the ability  to notice what's happening in the  language which all good language  learners have the fourth thing was to  focus on words vocabulary rather than  grammar now the fifth secret is to be  patient patient I see more frustrated  language learners who get upset because  they forget words they get upset because  they don't understand even after  listening many many times through the  same content they certain parts of it  they just don't understand or they watch  a movie many times and they can't  understand it let me tell you that is  absolutely normal and you will have and  continue to have times when you find it  difficult to say what you want to say  and maybe the day before you felt we  were doing very well and all of a sudden  you can't say what you want to say and  that is absolutely normal it's important  to realize that the brain is constantly  learning and there are a number of books  that I've read about how the brain  learns the brain will constantly learn  and it's constantly changing and  renewing itself however it does so on  its own schedule so just because you  studied something doesn't mean you're  going to learn it however you have to be  confident that as you continue and  persevere in your studies and if you're  doing things that you like to do which  in my case is listening and reading and  even if I'm talking to people of course  I'm listening and I'm noticing all of  this is gradually improving my  capability in the language so you have  to accept that and it's not going to be  overnight and it may take six months for  certain  things to sink in but all of a sudden  they do and almost without realizing it  and I've had this feeling all of a  sudden I'll go back to a text that I  struggled with to three months four  months earlier and it seemed that I  never made any progress I could never  understand certain parts of it and all  of a sudden lo and behold it's crystal  clear to me and similarly in speaking  you have these moments of great triumph  when you were in a discussion and lo and  behold you were able to express your  ideas just the way you wanted to or  almost and maybe the next day you won't  be quite so successful but it's a very  gradual process it's not obvious which  words or which you know structures in  the language the brain is going to learn  first or later so you just have to be  patient and you have to believe that  what you're doing is going to lead to  the desired result so anytime you get  frustrated upset because you forgot  something or because you don't  understand something all these negative  thoughts are very damaging to the  learning process again I'm not a  neuroscientist but there is so much of  sort of emotion in in how the brain  learns that it's very important not to  get negative and to be patient and  realize that it's a long road hopefully  an enjoyable road but one that will  definitely lead to fluency in that  language but fluency need not mean  perfection so if you don't expect  perfection but you do expect to  constantly improve then I think you can  afford to be patient so that's my fifth  secret be patient thank you for  listening
 

